        "headingPashto": "برخه 4: د قرطبې جومات او علامه اقبال",
        "text": "In 1931, during his visit to Europe for the Round Table Conferences, Allama Muhammad Iqbal was granted special permission by the Spanish government to offer prayers inside the Masjid of Cordoba—the first Muslim prayer heard within its walls since 1492. Iqbal was so moved by the magnificence of the masjid that he saw in its stones the living traces of the 'Ishq' of those who built it.\n\nIshq, according to Iqbal, is not mere romantic infatuation; it is the burning divine desire, selfless dedication, and moral passion to achieve perfection and self-realization in accordance with the teachings of Islam. It was this transformative spiritual energy that inspired early Muslims to construct everlasting monuments of faith, intellect, and justice. Iqbal immortalized this experience in his celebrated philosophical masterpiece poem 'Masjid-e-Qurtuba' in Bal-e-Jibril:\n\n'Yet there exists a hue of eternity in this imprint\nThe one that has been completed by some man of God;\nThe action of the Man of God gets accelerated with Ishq,\nThe essence of life is Ishq, death is forbidden for it.'\n\nIqbal attributed the eternal majesty of the Masjid to this intense spiritual fire: 'Your grandeur and beauty manifest the man of God; he is eminent and handsome, you too are eminent and handsome.' For Iqbal, the monument stands as an enduring call for the moral awakening and renaissance of the Muslim Ummah.",
