        "text": "At the dawn of the twentieth century, when the political destiny of Muslims in the Subcontinent was at a formative stage, most Muslim women led secluded lives within their homes. Bound by strict social conservatism and centuries-old cultural customs, they were largely excluded from public life and political discourse. A handful of visionary women, however, took the initial courageous steps during the Khilafat Movement (1919–1922)—a pan-Islamic mass movement launched for the restoration of the Ottoman Caliphate. The name of Abadi Begum, popularly revered as 'Bi Amma', stands foremost among these pioneering figures. As the courageous mother of Maulana Muhammad Ali Jauhar and Maulana Shaukat Ali, she cast aside traditional seclusion to address crowded political gatherings across India, inspiring both men and women to sacrifice their wealth and freedom for national honor.",
