        "headingPashto": "برخه 4: د پاکستان په تحریک کې د ښځو رول",
        "text": "The diplomatic acumen of Muslim women proved instrumental on the world stage. In 1946, Quaid-e-Azam nominated Begum Jahanara Shahnawaz and M.A.H. Ispahani to undertake an international diplomatic mission to the United States. Their crucial mandate was to address delegations at the United Nations and dismantle hostile Congress propaganda that falsely portrayed the Pakistan demand as a reactionary theocratic movement. Begum Shahnawaz delivered eloquent, persuasive addresses that impressed international diplomats, winning global sympathy for the Muslim cause.\n\nDuring the historic 1947 Punjab Civil Disobedience Movement, when male leaders were imprisoned, women took to the streets in fearless defiance of British tear gas, baton charges, and mass arrests. Begum Salma Tassaduq Hussain, Fatima Begum, and brave student activists famously scaled the walls of the Lahore Civil Secretariat, pulled down the Union Jack, and hoisted the Muslim League green flag. When the Constituent Assembly of Pakistan convened, Begum Shahnawaz and Begum Shaista Ikramullah took their seats as pioneering female legislators, ensuring that the new nation was founded upon the unshakeable bedrock of gender equality.",
