eng10Unit6.exercise.comprehension=[
{question:'What are the things that thrive despite the ravages of war?',answer:'The poem names blooming lilacs, tulips, morning-glories, daisies, pansies, roses, gardens and lilies, while clouds and waves continue their movement.'},
{question:'What are some of the different words and images used in the poem that convey the emotion of loss?',answer:'Words such as war, death, suffering, hate, wrath, guns, cannon, shame and nightmare convey loss and pain; they are set against images of blooming flowers and continuing life.'},
{question:'In the poem “In Spite of War”, there are several examples of personification. Identify two examples of personification; explain what is being personified and how in each example.',answer:'The morning-glory says “Courage!” and the daisy murmurs “Rejoice!”, giving flowers the human ability to speak. The pansies lift their eyes to the speaker, giving flowers a human action.'},
{question:'Make a list of the words that tell you about the tone of the poem “In spite of War” and explain how those words tell us what the tone is. Use examples from the poem to back up your reasoning.',answer:'Words such as “laughs”, “sings”, “praise”, “Courage”, “Rejoice”, “divine”, “glory” and “ecstasy” create a hopeful, affirming tone despite the contrasting words “war”, “death”, “hate” and “nightmare”.'},
{question:'What is the theme of the poem “In spite of War”?',answer:'The poem presents the persistence of life, beauty and hope in nature and the human spirit despite war, suffering and death.'}
];
