eng10Unit8.sourcePages=[{pdf:'10th english.pdf',pdfPage:81,printedPage:'74',title:'Unit 8 learning outcomes',text:'Outcomes cover pre-reading prediction; identifying supporting details and paragraph organization; vocabulary roots and spelling; factual group presentations; critical responses, opinions and solutions; adverb positions/degrees; and adverbial phrases and clauses.'},{pdf:'10th english.pdf',pdfPage:82,printedPage:'75',title:'Women’s Role in the Pakistan Movement, opening',text:'Pre-reading asks about gender roles in society/community/home and women’s role in national development. Reading introduces early-twentieth-century seclusion, the Khilafat Movement (1919–1922), Abadi Begum (Bi Amma), her sons Muhammad Ali Jauhar and Shaukat Ali, Quaid-e-Azam’s support for women’s participation, Fatima Jinnah, the 1938 Patna Muslim League session and the All India Muslim Women’s Sub-Committee. Image captions identify Lahore Muslim League activists and prominent women.'},{pdf:'10th english.pdf',pdfPage:83,printedPage:'76',title:'Women’s Role in the Pakistan Movement, women leaders and mobilization',text:'Continues women leaders’ names and regional roles, Muslim Students Federation women’s wing, Begum Fatima’s mobilization of students and Quaid’s November 1942 address at Jinnah Islamic College for Girls, demonstrations after the 1946 Punjab election, and Jahanara Shahnawaz’s appointment to the Parliamentary Board.'},{pdf:'10th english.pdf',pdfPage:84,printedPage:'77',title:'Women’s Role in the Pakistan Movement, diplomacy and mass activism',text:'Continues Jahanara Shahnawaz and M.A.H. Ispahani’s 1946 international advocacy, women elected to the Constituent Assembly, women’s organizing in Peshawar and the Frontier, Lady Haroon’s 1945 tour, the 1947 flag-raising at the Punjab Secretariat, and source citation to Dushka H. Saiyid’s Muslim Women of the British Punjab.'},{pdf:'10th english.pdf',pdfPage:85,printedPage:'78',title:'Women’s Role glossary and comprehension',text:'Glossary: emancipation, social conservatism, secluded, game changer, revitalizing, mobilization, dispel, firebrand, chastise, valiant, hoist. Comprehension asks how Quaid-e-Azam empowered women, how Begum Fatima mobilized students, and why women were equal partners.'},{pdf:'10th english.pdf',pdfPage:86,printedPage:'79',title:'Women’s Role comprehension and MCQs',text:'Comprehension asks about women’s role and paragraph ideas/cause-effect. MCQs test secluded, galaxy, social revolution and causes of women’s expanded role.'},{pdf:'10th english.pdf',pdfPage:87,printedPage:'80',title:'Women’s Role roots and chronological writing',text:'MCQ asks paragraph order (chronological). Roots: anti=against, aster=star, aqua=water, auto=self, pel/pul=push/drive, bio=life. Word practice: automatic, anti-war, dispel, aqueous, astrology. Writing introduces chronological order.'},{pdf:'10th english.pdf',pdfPage:88,printedPage:'81',title:'Paragraph organization and adverb placement',text:'Explains order of importance, spatial order and specific-to-general. Learners reorganize the essay paragraphs, prepare a five-minute group presentation about diverse roles of Pakistani women, and position adverbs also, only, both, hardly and carefully.'}];
