{pdf:'10th english.pdf',pdfPage:114,printedPage:'107',title:'Unit 11 Genetically Modified Organisms learning outcomes',text:'Outcomes cover critical intensive reading, scanning, contextual vocabulary, unified paragraph writing with pronoun agreement/transitions and chronological/sequential/spatial order, sentence types, information sources, critical response, cause/consequence and solutions, and persuasive/argumentative essays distinguishing fact from opinion and persuasion from propaganda.'},
{pdf:'10th english.pdf',pdfPage:115,printedPage:'108',title:'Genetically Modified Organisms, opening',text:'Pre-reading asks learners to predict from images showing broiler weight growth (1957: 840g; 1978: 945g; 2005: 2202g) and poultry. Reading explains selective breeding, genetics, genes as DNA subunits, inheritance, genetically modified food/organisms, and genetic engineering by transplanting DNA from other organisms; text contrasts this with conventional selection and breeding.'},
{pdf:'10th english.pdf',pdfPage:116,printedPage:'109',title:'Genetic engineering history and coded process figure',text:'Continues the distinction from conventional breeding; notes 1946 discovery of DNA transfer between organisms, natural transfer mechanisms, antibiotic resistance in bacteria, the first genetically modified plant in 1983 (antibiotic-resistant tobacco), and China’s early-1990s commercialization of virus-resistant tobacco. Diagram labels: bacteria; DNA extraction and isolation; cloning and designing genes; transformation; cell and tissue culture; plant breeding. A case study describes Starlight Avatar, a bioluminescent engineered plant receiving marine-bacteria DNA in its chloroplast genome.'}
