{pdf:'10th english.pdf',pdfPage:121,printedPage:'114',title:'GMO glossary and comprehension',text:'Glossary concludes with luminescent, marine, genes, consequence and agribusiness. Seven comprehension questions ask for definitions of gene/genetics; why gene discovery was a turning point; genetic engineering and its role; examples and differences from natural organisms; two merits/demerits; future effects; and safe-use suggestions for genetically modified foods.'},
{pdf:'10th english.pdf',pdfPage:122,printedPage:'115',title:'GMO textbook multiple-choice questions',text:'Five MCQs ask what holds an organism’s information database (genes); the meaning of turning point; whether the 1946 DNA-transfer statement is fact/opinion/inference/argument; the text type; and the meaning of “manipulation of genes … was just science fiction”.'},
{pdf:'10th english.pdf',pdfPage:123,printedPage:'116',title:'GMO vocabulary inference, writing and speaking',text:'Vocabulary table asks learners to infer meanings from context, justify with textual evidence, give dictionary definitions and judge inference accuracy for breeding, manipulation, resistance, stimuli, luminescent and evaporates. Writing: argumentative essay “Truth Is Stranger than Fiction”; research genetic modifications in animals and write about Dolly the cloned sheep. Speaking: groups of five discuss possible effects of GM food on future generations.'},
{pdf:'10th english.pdf',pdfPage:124,printedPage:'117',title:'Grammar: sentence types and transformations',text:'Defines four sentence purposes: declarative statements, interrogative questions, imperative commands/suggestions/warnings and exclamatory strong emotion. Exercise classifies eight sentences. Shows affirmative/negative/exclamatory/interrogative transformations using “It was good of him to help the poor man” and “Shazia passed the test.”'}
